code. The paper reports that this conjecture has been proved for $n=2,3$n=2,3 and $n=5$n=5, while it remains open in general.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Peter Horak and Dongryul Kim, “50 Years of the Golomb–Welch Conjecture”, arXiv:1706.03589 (2018).\n\ncoding-theorylee-metricperfect-codes\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) The total Thurston-Bennequin number of complete and complete bipartite Legendrian graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/280538423_The_total_Thurston-Bennequin_number_of_complete_and_complete_bipartite_Legendrian_graphs)\nciteturn11search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.3 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Remarks about K 5. ... 6 DANIELLE O’DONNOL AND ELENA PAVELESCU ... So it can have cycles where the Bennequin inequality\nand the other two have tb =−1.\n\nRemark 2.4.The graph K 4 is adaptable [8], that is, given any set of seven knot types,\n\nthere exists an embedding of K 4 with its seven cycles realizing the seven knot types. All\n\ntopological embeddings of a graph have a Legendrian realization [6]. Since T B 4(K 4) =\n\nT B 3(K 4), if all cycles of K 4 are of the same knot type, L, with tb max(L)>0, then at\n\nleast one of the 3−cycles has non-maximal tb. On the other hand, if all cycles of K 4\n\nare of the same knot type, L, with tb max(L)<0, then at least one of the 4−cycles has\n\nnon-maximal tb.\n\n2.2. Remarks about K 5.We look at unknotted minimal Legendrian embeddings of\n\nK 5. Since K 5 has ten 3−cycles, Theorem 2.2 for n= 5 and r= 4 says\n\nT B 4(K 5) = 2 T B 3(K 5) = −20.\n\n6 DANIELLE O’DONNOL AND ELENA PAVELESCU\n\nEach of ﬁve K 4 subgraphs of K 5 contains exactly one 4−cycle with tb =−2. This means\n\nthat among the ﬁfteen 4−cycles of K 5, ten have tb =−1 and ﬁve have tb =−2.\n\nFor n= 5 and r= 5, Theorem 2.2 says\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiameter Perfect Lee Codes (https://arxiv.org/abs/1109.3475)\nciteturn11academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.1 years ago; Authors: Peter Horak, Bader F. ... Interest in these codes has been triggered by the Golomb-Welch conjecture on the existence of the perfect error-correcting Lee codes. ... $ This is in a strict contrast with perfect error-correcting Lee codes of word length $n$ over $Z\\,$\\ as there is a unique such code for $n=3,$ and its is conjectured that this is always the case when $2n+1$ is a prime.\nTitle: Diameter Perfect Lee Codes\nAuthors: Peter Horak, Bader F. AlBdaiwi\nDate: Thu Sep 15 20:19:22 2011\n\nLee codes have been intensively studied for more than 40 years. Interest in these codes has been triggered by the Golomb-Welch conjecture on the existence of the perfect error-correcting Lee codes. In this paper we deal with the existence and enumeration of diameter perfect Lee codes. As main results we determine all $q$ for which there exists a linear diameter-4 perfect Lee code of word length $n$ over $Z_{q},$ and prove that for each $n\\geq 3$ there are uncountable many diameter-4 perfect Lee codes of word length $n$ over $Z.$ This is in a strict contrast with perfect error-correcting Lee codes of word length $n$ over $Z\\,$\\ as there is a unique such code for $n=3,$ and its is conjectured that this is always the case when $2n+1$ is a prime. We produce diameter perfect Lee codes by an algebraic construction that is based on a group homomorphism. This will allow us to design an efficient algorithm for their decoding. We hope that this construction will turn out to be useful far beyond the scope of this paper.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEigenvalue bounds for the distance-t chromatic (https://backoffice.biblio.ugent.be/download/01KCK5AV8X13X56B1EQH5Y75CA/01KCK5GNVJBSGVBP8ABA